    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d366">

                <pb facs="0289" n="285" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg285"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.115/246</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Tientsin</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Caldwell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Ambassador
                        in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)<anchor n="76"
                        xml:id="d366-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 602</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tientsin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-02-28">February 28, 1938</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: As of possible interest to the Embassy, I
                    have the honor to enclose one copy of the Chinese original of a despatch
                    received today ostensibly from the “Headquarters of the First Route Army of the
                    Masses’ Anti-Japanese Forces in North China” at Nanmeng, Pahsien, Hopei,
                    together with an English translation prepared at this office,<anchor n="77"
                        xml:id="d366-f2"/> in which the Consulate General is requested to notify
                    Americans to display American flags after March 20 in order that they may be
                    given appropriate protection during positive operations against the Japanese
                    which will begin on that date. The cover in which this communication was
                    received indicates that it was mailed in Tientsin although the place of origin
                    noted on the outside of the envelope is “Nanmeng Church”.</p>
                <p>Nanmeng is situated about 50 miles south of Peiping and 30 miles west of Tientsin
                    and only a few miles north of Pahsien District city. The Consulate General has
                    received a number of reports during the past several months that representatives
                    of the 8th Route Army were busy in Pahsien and neighboring districts in an
                    effort to organize the people for guerrilla warfare against the Japanese.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. K. Caldwell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
